===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|party}}, {{m|enm|partye}}, {{m|enm|partie}}, from {{der|en|xno|partie}}, from {{der|en|fro|partie||side, part; portion, share; separation, division|lit=that which is divided}}, noun use of feminine of past participle of {{der|en|fro|partir|t=to divide, separate}}, from {{der|en|la|partire||to share, part, distribute, divide}}, from {{m|la|pars|t=a part, piece, a share}}; see also [[part]]. {{etydate|c|1300}}. {{doublet|en|partita}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> {{surf|+suf|en|part|y|pos2=noun suffix}}.
* {{senseno|en|Communist Party|t=the [[Communist Party]]|cap=1}} is a {{semantic loan|en|ru|партия|nocap=1}}, short for {{m|ru|Коммунистическая партия}}. {{rfv-etym|en}}

====Noun====
[[File:Birthday party.jpg|thumb|A [[birthday]] '''party''' ''({{senseno|en|fun|etym=false}}'') for a child.]]

{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[person]] or [[group]] of people constituting one side in a legal proceeding, such as in a [[legal action]] or a [[contract]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1300s}}
#: {{ux|en|The contract requires that the '''party''' of the first part pay the fee.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1612|author=w:Sir John Davies|title=Discoverie of the True Causes why Ireland was never entirely subdued
|passage=if the Jury had found that the '''party''' slain had been of English race and nation, it had been adjudged felony}}
# {{lb|en|dated|slang|vulgar|or|humorous}} A [[person]]; an [[individual]]. {{defdate|from after 1475}}
#: {{ux|en|He is a queer '''party'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|page=135|passage='These young '''parties''' have a way of looking at one, sir,' he would say apologetically, 'which I don't call respectable.'}}
## With '''[[to]]''': someone who takes part in an [[action]] or [[affair]]; an [[accessory]].
##: {{ux|en|I can't possibly be (a) [[party to|'''party''' to]] that kind of reckless behaviour.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare|_|in general sense}} A [[group]] of people forming one side in a given [[dispute]], [[contest]], etc., or united in maintaining a [[cause]], [[policy]], or [[opinion]] in opposition to others; a [[faction]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1320s}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Tarzan of the Apes|6|passage=A mile back in the forest the tribe had heard the fierce challenge of the gorilla, and, as was his custom when any danger threatened, Kerchak called his people together, partly for mutual protection against a common enemy, since this gorilla might be but one of a '''party''' of several, and also to see that all members of the tribe were accounted for.}}
## {{senseid|en|political group}}{{lb|en|politics}} A [[political]] [[group]] considered as a formal whole, usually organized on a [[national]] basis, which contests [[elections]] and aims to form or take part in a [[government]]; a [[political party]]. {{defdate|from 1680s}}
##: {{ux|en|The green '''party''' took 12% of the vote.}}
##: {{ux|en|His '''party''' was campaigning successfuly.}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=A Lady in Company|page=21|text="A fine man, that Dunwody, yonder," commented the young captain, as they parted, and as he turned to his prisoner. "We'll see him on in Washington some day.{{...}}A strong man&mdash;a strong one; and a heedless." ¶ "Of what '''party''' is he?" she inquired, as though casually.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Nineteen Eighty-Four|chapter=7|page=76|passage=The '''Party''' told you to reject the evidence of your eyes and ears. It was their final, most essential command.}}
##* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1996
|author=w:John Gittings
|chapter=Peasants in Revolt
|title=Real China: From Cannibalism to Karaoke
|url=https://archive.org/details/realchinafromcan0000gitt/
|publisher=w:Pocket Books
|year_published=1997
|ISBN=0-671-51651-5
|OCLC=38996810
|page=46
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/realchinafromcan0000gitt/page/46/
|text=In the freer political atmosphere before the 1989 repression, peasant protests against the '''Party''' in Henan were sometimes openly discussed. A transcript of a meeting between peasants and cadres in Jili district near Luoyang published in the ''Peasants’ Daily'' recorded some vivid complaints.}}
##* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Fredreka Schouten and Arit John|title=The DNC’s cash crunch deepens as new filings show Republicans with a huge advantage|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/21/politics/dnc-financial-troubles-fec-filings|date=2025-06-21|passage=The RNC entered June with about $72.4 million in cash reserves, nearly five times the $15 million stockpiled by the DNC, as the two '''parties''' gear up for gubernatorial races this fall and next year’s costly midterm elections for Congress. {{...}} The '''party'''’s cash crunch is serious enough that '''party''' officials have weighed seeking a line of credit to help it stay afloat, a development first reported by The New York Times.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250816035510/https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/21/politics/dnc-financial-troubles-fec-filings|archivedate=16 August 2025}}
### {{senseid|en|Communist Party}} {{lb|en|with '''{{m|en|the}}'''|&|capital initial}} The [[Communist Party]], especially as the sole [[political party]] of a communist state. {{defdate|from 1919}}
### {{lb|en|dated|now|_|rare}} The feeling of [[solidarity]] with and [[support]] for one's [[political party]]; [[partisanship]], especially in political matters. {{defdate|from 1720s}}
## {{lb|en|RPG|online gaming}} Active [[player character]]s organized into a single group.
## {{lb|en|video games}} A group of characters controlled by the [[player]].
# {{senseid|en|military}} {{lb|en|military}} A [[detachment]] of troops selected for a particular [[service]] or [[duty]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1330}}
#: {{ux|en|[[boarding party|boarding '''party''']]}}
#: {{ux|en|The settlers were attacked early next morning by a scouting '''party'''.}}
# A [[group]] of people [[gather]]ed together, especially temporarily, for a specific [[purpose]] such as [[travel]] or [[sport]]. {{defdate|from after 1387}}
#: {{ux|en|We're expecting a large '''party''' from the London office.}}
#: {{ux|en|Do you have a table available for a '''party''' of four?}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|volume=II|chapter=IX|passage='{{...}}I received it on the very morning of our intended '''party''' to Whitwell {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=I|chapter=XV|pages=163–164|pageref=163|passage={{...}} the whole '''party''' were still standing and talking together very agreeably, when the sound of horses drew their notice, and Darcy and Bingley were seen riding down the street.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=V|page=63|passage=We made an odd '''party''' before the arrival of the Ten, particularly when the Celebrity dropped in for lunch or dinner.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=May and June|title=Nature Provides a Railway Tunnel|journal=Railway Magazine|page=132|text=When the line was being surveyed in 1880, the survey '''party''', proceeding along the course of Stock Creek, reached a natural amphitheatre with a rock wall 200 ft. high, in the face of which there was an arched tunnel entrance, [...] into which the stream passed.}}
## {{senseid|en|fun}} A [[social]] [[gathering]], usually of invited [[guests]], which typically involves [[eating]], [[drinking]], and [[entertainment]] and often held to [[celebrate]] a particular occasion. {{defdate|from 1700s}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm throwing a huge '''party''' for my 21st birthday.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|date=Apr 3 2016|actor=w:John Oliver|title=w:Last Week Tonight with John Oliver|season= 3|number= 7| episode=Congressional Fundraising |network=HBO|text=So-So, tonight, tonight, let’s look at where that time actually goes, and let’s begin with the most obvious form of fundraising: fundraisers. These are usually shitty '''parties''' in D.C. bars, restaurants, or townhouses, and there are a lot of them! The Sunlight Foundation estimates that, in the last election cycle, members of Congress held over 28 hundred fundraisers! Washington is like Rod Stewart’s haircut: '''party''' in the front, '''party''' in the back, frankly too much '''party''' and no business anywhere to be found!}}
##* {{quote-song|en|year=2017|artist=Feist|album=Pleasure|title=Any Party|passage=You know I’d leave any '''party''' for you / 'Cause no party’s so sweet as a party of two}}
## A gathering of acquaintances so that one of them may [[offer]] items for [[sale]] to the rest of them.
##: {{ux|en|Tupperware '''party'''}}
##: {{ux|en|lingerie '''party'''}}
# A small group of [[birds]] or [[mammals]]. {{defdate|from after 1387}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=5 Mar 1830|journal=The Australian|location=Sydney|page=3|column=3|text=Small '''parties''' of cattle were seen browsing in places where they must with with difficulty have kept their footing[.]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=D.D. Cunningham|title=Some Indian Friends and Acquaintances|publisher=John Murray|location=London|page=28|passage=A '''party''' of mynas, consisting of several males and females, once selected the top of a low terraced roof, just below my verandah, as a site for courting and quarrelling.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Mark Brazil|title=The Birds of East Asia|publisher=Christopher Helm|location=London|page=272|passage=Small '''parties''' and flocks close to breeding sites commonly give shrill, high-pitched, trilling screams.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[part]] or [[portion]]. {{defdate|ca. 1300|1654}}
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur| book=II| chapter=xv| passage=And so the moost '''party''' of the castel that was falle doune thorugh that dolorous stroke laye vpon Pellam and balyn thre dayes.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A prospective [[partner]] or an [[offer]] of marriage. {{defdate|1492|1854}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[decision]], [[resolution]], [[agreement]]. {{defdate|1585|1767}}

