#: {{ux|en|a '''strong''' verb}}
# {{lb|en|Indo-European studies|especially of {{lg|stem}}s or inflected {{lg|root}}s}} Of a form in which the [[accent]] is in the [[leftmost]] (first) [[slot]] allowed in the relevant [[paradigm]], or reflecting an ancestral [[Proto-Indo-European]] form in which this was the case, as opposed to tending to shift to the right.
#* {{quote-book|en|last=Clackson|first=James|authorlink=James Clackson|year=2007|title=Indo-European Linguistics: An Introduction|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=978-0-511-36609-3|section=3.5|page=79|text=Paradigmatic slots in which the accent and correlating e-grade are positioned further to the left of the word are traditionally termed '''''strong''''', those with accent and e-grade further to the right, are called ''weak''. In all nouns and verbs which show these alternations, the '''strong''' and the weak forms are predictable from the morphological category […].|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=DJDjNp6wODoC&q=strong}}
#: {{ant|en|weak}}
#: {{ux|en|[[PIE]] '''strong''' stems were most frequently in the {{lg|full grade}} ([[e-grade]]), less often in the {{m|en|o-grade}} (though more common in [[thematic]] nouns and [[stative]] verbs) and least often in the {{lg|zero grade}}.}}
## {{lb|en|of {{lg|noun}}s and {{lg|adjective}}s}} In the [[nominative]], [[accusative]] or [[vocative]] [[case]], regardless of [[number]], or sometimes in the [[locative]] [[singular]].
