swing.content:
{{also|Swing}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|swyngen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|swingan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*swingan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*swinganą}} (compare {{cog|nds|swingen}}, {{cog|de|schwingen}}, {{cog|nl|zwingen}}, {{cog|sv|svinga}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*swenk-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*sweng-}} (compare {{cog|gd|seang|t=thin}}). Related to {{m|en|swink}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈswɪŋ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-swing.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-swing.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋ|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|swung,swang<l:archaic,or,dialectal>|+,swungen<l:archaic>}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To rotate about an [[off-centre]] [[fixed]] [[point]].
#: {{troponyms|en|pivot|swivel}}
#: {{ux|en|The plant '''swung''' in the breeze.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Tarzan of the Apes|12|passage=With one accord the tribe '''swung''' rapidly toward the frightened cries, and there found Terkoz holding an old female by the hair and beating her unmercifully with his great hands.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|isbn= 9781448136926 |title=Star Wars: Fate of the Jedi: Apocalypse| author=Troy Denning| page=3| date=29 February 2012| publisher=w:Random House| url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=hA3ORUcrvBQC&dq=wung&source=gbs_navlinks_s| passage={{smallcaps|The starliner '''swung''' into orbit around the planet Coruscant,}} and beyond the observation bubble appeared a glittering expanse of a billion golden lights. Through a thousand centuries of strife, those lights continued to shine.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Rob Smyth|title=Pep Guardiola comes out swinging haymakers in all directions|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/feb/10/football-daily-email-manchester-city-pep-guardiola|date=2023-02-10|passage=Guardiola '''swung''' haymakers in all directions; he presented one of the world’s richest clubs as a noble, put-upon underdog; and he established a siege mentality with such coruscating precision that Football Daily instantly bet the farm on City winning the Premier League this season.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230320183230/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/feb/10/football-daily-email-manchester-city-pep-guardiola}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[dance]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[ride]] on a swing.
#: {{ux|en|The children laughed as they '''swung'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|sex}} To participate in the [[swinging]] lifestyle; to participate in [[wifeswapping]].
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Anonymous|title=My life in sex: ‘We have rules for swinging: no one cheating on their wives, no one who supports Trump’|date=2017-10-13|url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2017/oct/13/my-sex-life-the-swinging-couple|passage= We find it difficult to meet couples our age, and often '''swing''' with single, straight men. We have rules: no married guys cheating on their wives, no one too young or too old, and no one who supports Trump.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180217103722/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2017/oct/13/my-sex-life-the-swinging-couple}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Eleanor Gordon-Smith|quotee=anonymous|title=My husband persuaded me to swing. I’m desperate to do it again, he’s not – what should I do?|date=2023-10-26|url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2023/oct/27/my-husband-persuaded-me-to-swing-im-desperate-to-do-it-again-hes-not-what-should-i-do|passage=My husband wasn’t so lucky and didn’t get to '''swing''' at all so was very disappointed. I’m desperate to do it all again but he’s not so keen.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20231216084418/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2023/oct/27/my-husband-persuaded-me-to-swing-im-desperate-to-do-it-again-hes-not-what-should-i-do}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[hang]] from the [[gallows]]; to be punished by hanging, [[swing for]] something or someone; (often hyperbolic) to be severely punished.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:die by hanging}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|author=Arthur Conan Doyle|title=The Red-Headed League| passage=“It's all clear,” he whispered. “Have you the chisel and the bags? Great Scott! Jump, Archie, jump, and I'll '''swing''' for it!”<br>Sherlock Holmes had sprung out and seized the intruder by the collar. The other dived down the hole, and I heard the sound of rending cloth as Jones clutched at his skirts.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|cricket}} {{qualifier|of a bowler}} To make the [[ball]] move sideways in its [[trajectory]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket|of a ball}} To move sideways in its trajectory.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[fluctuate]] or [[change]].
#: {{ux|en|It wasn't long before the crowd's mood '''swung''' towards restless irritability.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To move (an object) backward and forward; to [[wave]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''swung''' his sword as hard as he could.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[change]] (a numerical [[result]]); especially to change the [[outcome]] of an [[election]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To make (something) work; especially to [[afford]] (something) [[financially]].
#: {{ux|en|If it’s not too expensive, I think we can '''swing''' it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|music}} To play [[notes]] that are in pairs by making the first of the pair slightly longer than written ([[augment]]ation) and the second shorter, resulting in a bouncy, uneven [[rhythm]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|and|intransitive|boxing}} To move one's arm in a [[punch]]ing motion.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} In dancing, to turn around in a small circle with one's partner, holding hands or arms.
#: {{ux|en|"to '''swing''' one's partner", or simply "to '''swing'''"}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|engineering}} To admit or turn something for the purpose of [[shape|shaping]] it; said of a [[lathe]].
#: {{ux|en|The lathe can '''swing''' a pulley of 12 inches diameter.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|carpentry}} To put (a door, gate, etc.) on hinges so that it can swing or turn.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To turn round by action of wind or tide when at anchor.
#: {{ux|en|A ship '''swings''' with the tide.}}
# To turn in a different direction.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 November 30|author=Paul Bigland|title=Destination Oban: a Sunday in Scotland|journal=RAIL|issue=971|page=75|text=Soon after departure, we cross the invisible border into Scotland to enjoy more stunning coastal scenery, before the line finally '''swings''' inland at Burnmouth to traverse pine-clad valleys, shadowed by the A1 trunk road until we rejoin the coast at Cove, east of Dunbar.}}
# To be [[sexually]] [[oriented]].
#: {{ux|en|[['''swing''' both ways]]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=12 Feb 77|volume=4|number=33|journal=Gay Community News|page=9|title=Theatre Profiles Part II|author=Don Shewey;John Glines|text="The Jumping Place," the second play we did, was directed by the author, and the whole cast of seven was straight. We don't ask when somebody comes here how they '''swing''', only that they commit themselves to the principles of the theatre.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To engage in sexual activity with a man (specifically, with his penis)
#: {{ux|en|She couldn't wait to get out of the restaurant with him, so she could take him back to her place and '''swing''' on his dick all night.}}

