====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{senseid|en|to temporarily transfer employment}} {{lb|en|transitive|UK}} To transfer temporarily to alternative employment.
#: {{syn|en|detail#Verb}}
#: {{ux|en|The army officer was '''seconded''' while he held civil office.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=October|title=Talking of Trains: Last of the M.S.W.J.R.|journal=Trains Illustrated|pages=585–586|text=Things changed quickly from 1892 when Sam Fay was '''seconded''' from the L.S.W.R. as General Manager & Secretary.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author={{w|Paul Leonard (writer)|Paul Leonard}}|title=w:Dreamstone Moon|chapter=9|passage=Daniel had still been surprised, however, to find the lab area deserted, all the scientists apparently '''seconded''' by Cleomides's military friends.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2026|author=Michael Falk|title=Wikilambda the ultimate: the Wikimedia foundation’s search for the perfect language|journal=AI & Society: Knowledge, Culture and Communication|doi=10.1007/s00146-026-02899-w|text=As ''The Signpost'' reported, all was not well with Wikilambda. Ten days prior to the article, a group of Google employees '''seconded''' to Abstract Wikipedia had released a damning report on Meta-Wiki, the site where projects of the Wikimedia Foundation are documented.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[assist]] or [[support]]; to [[back]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|IV|i|91|1|passage=Wee haue Supplyes, to '''ſecond''' our Attempt:{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Man|I|6|line=61|passage=In human works, tho’ labour’d on with pain, / A thouſand movements ſcarce one purpoſe gain; / In ''God's'', one ſingle can ''its End'' produce, / Yet ſerves to '''ſecond''' too ſome ''other Uſe''.}}
# {{rfm-sense|en}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[agree]] as a second person to (a proposal), usually to reach a necessary [[quorum]] of [[two]]. (This may come from [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]] above.)
#: {{ux|en|I '''second''' the motion.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|music}} To accompany by singing as the second performer.

