# To make or form a [[#Noun|cross]].
## To place across or athwart; to cause to intersect.
##: {{ux|en|She frowned and '''crossed''' her arms.}}
## To lay or draw something across, such as a line.
##: {{ux|en|to '''cross''' the letter t}}
## To mark with an X.
##: {{ux|en|'''Cross''' the box which applies to you.}}
## To write lines of text at right angles to and over the top of one another in order to save paper.({{pedia|Crossed letter|nodot=1}})
##* {{RQ:Christie Autobiography|I|4|passage=An indulgent playmate, Grannie would lay aside the long scratchy-looking letter she was writing (heavily '''crossed''' ‘to save notepaper’) and enter into the delightful pastime of ‘a chicken from Mr Whiteley's’.}}
## {{lb|en|reflexive|to cross oneself}} To make the [[sign of the cross]] over oneself.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1846|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=w:Mosses from an Old Manse|chapter=Rappaccini's Daughter|passage=Again Beatrice '''crossed herself''' and sighed heavily as she bent over the dead insect.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make the sign of the cross over (something or someone).
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=298|passage="Well, no! that's what I cannot make out either," said the mother quite innocently, "for I've had castor in the cradle, - I have '''crossed''' him, and I put a silver brooch in his shirt, and I stuck a knife in the beam over the door, so I don't know how they could have managed to change him."}}
## {{lb|en|UK|Oxford University|slang|obsolete|transitive}} To mark a cross against the name of (a student) in the [[buttery]] or [[kitchen]], so that they cannot get food there.
##* '''2022''', Andrew Lang, ''Oxford''
##*: The reign of Mary was scarcely more favourable to letters. No one knew what to be at in religion. In Magdalen no one could be found to say Mass, the fellows were turned out, the undergraduates were whipped — boyish martyrs — and '''crossed''' at the buttery.
# To move relatively.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To go from one side of (something) to the other.
##: {{ux|en|Why did the chicken '''cross''' the road?}}
##: {{ux|en|You need to '''cross''' the street at the lights.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=8|passage=Now we plunged into a deep shade with the boughs lacing each other overhead, and '''crossed''' dainty, rustic bridges over the cold trout-streams, the boards giving back the clatter of our horses' feet: or anon we shot into a clearing, with a colored glimpse of the lake and its curving shore far below us.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 19, 2012|author=Phil McNulty| work=BBC Sport| title=England 1-0 Ukraine|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/18181971| passage=Ukraine, however, will complain long and hard about a contentious second-half incident when Marko Devic's shot clearly '''crossed''' the line before it was scrambled away by John Terry, only for the officials to remain unmoved.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 December 29|author=Philip Haigh|title=Rail's role in unifying Great Britain and Northern Ireland|journal=RAIL|issue=947|page=24|text=Whatever the merits or otherwise of Scottish independence or a united UK, plenty of people '''cross''' the border every year.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[travel]] in a [[direction]] or path that will intersect with that of another.
##: {{ux|en|Ships '''crossing''' from [[starboard]] have right-of-way.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To pass, as objects going in an opposite direction at the same time.
##* '''November 4, 1866''', {{w|James David Forbes}}, ''letter to E. C. Batten Esq.''
##*: Your kind letter '''crossed''' mine.
## {{lb|en|sports}} Relative movement by a player or of players.
### {{lb|en|cricket|reciprocally}} Of both batsmen, to pass each other when running between the [[wicket]]s in order to score [[run]]s.
### {{lb|en|football}} To pass the ball from one side of the pitch to the other side.
###: {{ux|en|He '''crossed''' the ball into the penalty area.}}
### {{lb|en|rugby}} To score a [[try]].
###* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 12, 2011|author=Mark Orlovac|work=BBC| title=England 59-13 Italy|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/rugby_union/9393726.stm| passage=England cut loose at the end of the half, Ashton, Mark Cueto and Mike Tindall all '''crossing''' before the break.}}
# {{lb|en|social}} To oppose.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[contradict]] (another) or [[frustrate]] the plans of.
##: {{ux|en|"You'll rue the day you tried to '''cross''' me, Tom Hero!" bellowed the villain.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens David Copperfield|passage=At length I begged him, with all the earnestness I felt, to tell me what had occurred to '''cross''' him so unusually, and to let me sympathize with him, if I could not hope to advise him.}}
##* {{quote-song|en|title=Gangsta's Paradise|text=But I ain't never '''crossed''' a man that didn't deserve it / Me be treated like a punk, you know that's unheard of / You better watch how you talkin' and where you walkin' / Or you and your homies might be lined in chalk|year=1995|artist=Coolio; L.V.|album=Gangsta's Paradise|lyricist=Artis Ivey, Jr.; Karry Sanders; Doug Rasheed|publisher=Tommy Boy|oclc=35267516|format=CD}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[interfere]] and [[cut off]] ; to [[debar]].
