==Translingual==

===Etymology===
{{clip|mul|en:'''Ill'''anun}}.

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol}}

# {{ISO 639|3}}

===See also===
* {{langcat}}

==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|ille||evil; wicked}}, from {{der|en|non|íllr|pos=adj}}, {{m|non|ílla|pos=adv}}, {{m|non|ílt|pos=n}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*ilhilaz}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁elḱ-}} (whence {{cog|la|ulcus||sore}}, {{cog|grc|ἕλκος||wound, ulcer}}, {{cog|sa|अर्शस्|tr=árśas||hemorrhoids}}).<ref>Michiel de Vaan, ''Etymological Dictionary of Latin and the Other Italic Languages'', s.v. "ulcus" (Leiden: Brill, 2008), 637.</ref>
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|-}} and {{cog|yol|ill}}, {{cog|da|ilde|t=bad}}, {{cog|fo|-}}, {{cog|is|illur|t=bad, ill, wicked}}, {{cog|nb|-}}, {{cog|nn|ille|t=bad}}, {{cog|sv|illa|t=badly; poorly}}.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ill.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪl|s=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|iller,more}}<!--"worse"/"worst" are not forms of "ill", even if they're usually used instead of "iller"/"illest". The OED does not agree with that analysis.-->

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[evil|Evil]]; [[wicked]] (of people). {{defdate|13th|19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=December 6 1709|author=w:Francis Atterbury|title=A Sermon Preached before the Sons of the Clergy, at their Anniversary-Meeting, in the Church of St. Paul
|passage=St. Paul chose to magnify his office when '''ill''' men conspired to lessen it.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|passage=A man who is conscious of having an '''ill''' character, cannot justly be angry with those who neglect and slight him.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Morally reprehensible (of behaviour etc.); [[blameworthy]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=w:George R.R. Martin|title=w:A Clash of Kings|publisher=Bantam|year_published=2011|page=2|passage=‘Go bring her. It is '''ill''' to keep a lady waiting.’}}
# Indicative of [[unkind]] or [[malevolent]] intentions; [[harsh]], [[cruel]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|He suffered from '''ill''' treatment.}}
# Unpropitious, unkind, faulty, not up to reasonable standard.
#: {{ux|en|'''ill''' manners; '''ill''' will}}
#* {{RQ:Heyer Unknown Ajax|chapter=1
|passage={{...}}his lordship was out of humour. That was the way Chollacombe described as knaggy an old gager as ever Charles had had the '''ill'''-fortune to serve. Stiff-rumped, that's what he was, always rubbing the rust, or riding grub, like he had been for months past.}}
# {{U|unwell}} in terms of [[health]] or physical condition; [[sick]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|mentally '''ill''' people}}
#: {{ux|en|I've been '''ill''' with the flu for the past few days.}}
#: {{ux|en|Sue made herself '''ill''' through overwork.}}
