===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=food|:inh|enm:bred|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰrewh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|bred}}, {{m|enm|breed}}, from {{inh|en|ang|brēad|t=fragment, bit, morsel, crumb", also "bread}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*braud}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*braudą|t=bread}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰrewh₁-|*bʰerw-, *bʰrewh₁-|t=to boil; to brew}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*bʰer-|t=to bear, carry}}. Alternatively, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*braudaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*brauþaz|t=broken piece, fragment}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰera-|t=to split, beat, hew, struggle}} (see {{l|en|brittle}}). Perhaps a conflation of the two. Possibly a {{doublet|en|broa|nocap=1}}. 
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|breid|t=bread}}, {{cog|yol|breed|t=bread}}, {{cog|frr|bruad}}, {{m|frr|Bruar}}, {{m|frr|brüüdj|t=bread}}, {{cog|stq|Brood|t=bread}}, {{cog|fy|brea|t=bread}}, {{cog|gsw|brot}}, {{m|gsw|broud}}, {{m|gsw|bruat}}, {{m|gsw|bròt}}, {{m|gsw|bröt|t=bread}}, {{cog|cim|proat}}, {{m|cim|pròat|t=bread}}, {{cog|nl|brood|t=bread}}, {{cog|de|Brot|t=bread}}, {{cog|nds-de|Brod}}, {{m|nds-de|Brood}}, {{m|nds-de|Broot}}, {{m|nds-de|Brot}}, {{m|nds-de|Bräot|t=bread}}, {{cog|li|broed|t=bread}}, {{cog|lb|Brout|t=bread}}, {{cog|mhn|proat|t=bread}}, {{cog|wym|brūt|t=bread; loaf}}, {{cog|yi|ברויט|t=bread}}, {{cog|da,nb|brød|t=bread}}, {{cog|ovd|broð|t=bread}}, {{cog|fo| breyð|t=bread}}, {{cog|is|brauð|t=bread}}, {{cog|nrn|brau}}, {{m|nrn|brow|t=bread}}, {{cog|nn|braud}}, {{m|nn|brød|t=bread}}, {{cog|sv|bröd|t=bread}}, {{cog|gme-cgo|broe|t=bread}}; also {{cog|kw|brys|t=thought; mind}}, {{cog|ga,gd|beir|t=bear, give birth to}}, {{cog|cy|bryd|t=aim, intent}}, {{cog|la|fors|t=chance, luck}}, {{cog|el|φέρνω}}, {{m|el|φέρω|t=to bear, carry}}, {{cog|sq|brydh|t=to ripen, soften; to crumble}}, {{cog|lv|bērt|t=to pour; to scatter, strew}}, {{cog|lt|berti|t=to scatter, strew}}, {{cog|be|бру́ха|t=belly}}, {{cog|cs|břich}}, {{m|cs|břicho}}, {{m|cs|břuch|t=belly}}, {{cog|csb|brzëch|t=belly}}, {{cog|pl|brzuch}}, {{m|pl|brzucho|t=belly}}, {{cog|ru|брю́хо|t=belly}}, {{cog|sk|brucho|t=belly}}, {{cog|hy|բերել|t=to bring, fetch}}, {{cog|fa|بردن|tr=bordan/burdan|t=to bear, carry}}, {{cog|xto,txb|pär-|t=to bear; to wear}}, {{cog|sa|भारयति|t=to carry}}. 
{{col-bottom}}
Eclipsed non-native {{noncog|enm|payn|t=bread}}, borrowed from {{noncog|fro|pain|t=bread}}. In this sense, mostly replaced {{m|en|loaf}}, which had been the more common term in Old English (see {{m|ang|hlāf}}), a process which similarly occured in other languages such as German.

