===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-fish and chips.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
[[Image:Fish and chips.jpg|thumb|Fish and chips]]
{{en-noun|head=[[fish]] [[and]] [[chip]]s|sp|attr=fish and chip|attr2=fish-and-chip}}

# A meal of [[fish]] [[fry|fried]] in [[batter]] and served with [[chip]]s (fried [[potato]] pieces), popular throughout the [[Anglosphere]], especially in coastal regions.
#: {{syn|en|fish and chippies<l:colloquial,or,childish,rare>}}
#: {{ux|en|Let's go to Sophie's by the sea; they have the most delicious '''fish and chips'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=18 Sep 1897|journal=The Age|location=Melbourne|page=9|column=5|text=Fried '''Fish and Chips''', two iron furnaces and chipping machine, low rent.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Pamela Sykes|title=Phoebe’s Family|location=Nashville, Tenn.|publisher=[[w:Thomas Nelson (publisher)|Thomas Nelson Inc.]]|year=1974|page=52|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/phoebesfamily0000syke/page/52/mode/1up|isbn=0-8407-6381-6|passage=“Perfectly splendid,” Miss Flower burbled on. “And a perfect contrast to Doris, who’s so pale and thin.” / “Too many '''fish and chips''' and not enough proper vitamins,” said Mrs. Smethers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Frances Thomas|chapter=22|title=Zak|location=Oxford, Oxon|publisher=[[w:Heinemann (publisher)|Heinemann Educational Books Ltd]]|series=New Windmill Series|year=1984|year_published=1988|page=117|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/zak0000thom/page/117/mode/1up|isbn=0 435 12335 1|passage=I manage to swallow a few chips just to convince them I’m not going to die of malnutrition but it’s hard going, I can tell you. Though normally '''fish and chips''' is my favourite.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gloria Hatrick|title=Masks|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Grolier#Orchard Books|Orchard Books]]|year=1992|year_published=1996|page=5|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/masks00hatr/page/5/mode/1up|isbn=0-531-09514-2|passage=Jeez, Will, you’ve definitely had too much '''fish and chips'''. Or maybe it was all those potato chips.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Food Journeys of a Lifetime: 500 Extraordinary Places to Eat Around the Globe|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=w:National Geographic Society|year=2009|page=157|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/foodjourneysofli0000unse/page/157/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4262-0507-1|passage=Newspaper wrapping is old news; most '''fish and chips''' are now served wrapped in plain white paper.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Craig LaBan|title=GoodTaste|journal=w:The Philadelphia Inquirer|volume_plain=181st year|issue=101|date=9 September 2010|page=F5|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/199572176/|passage=Co-owned by Ed Strojan and his step-father, Gary Coleman, who also has the English Gardener gift shop across the street, virtually everything here is made with an eye to authenticity, from the shortcakey scones with imported clotted cream to pastry-wrapped banger sausages, sides of mushy peas, Madras curry sauce, and, especially, those '''fish and chips'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Craig Stevenson; John Mackay|title=Inn Aff the Bar|location=East Kilbride|publisher=Cheapwayround Publishers|year=2013|page=189|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/innaffbar0000stev/page/189/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-9570252-3-3|passage=Craig was feeling better and we ordered two '''fish and chips''', only to be told that it would be at least an hour.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Keith Greenwood|chapter=Awakening|title=The Clueless Dead|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|publisher=w:Xlibris|year=2014|page=34|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PULnAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA34|isbn=978-1-4931-3750-3|passage=‘You feeling hungry, Mike?’ Ah, back to normality! / ‘Oh yes. I’ll just have a '''fish and chips''', Harry.’ / Two minutes later, he handed me the plate, and I retreated into my own private corner of the bar. The smell of warm potato chips and crumbed battered fish was welcoming, and it tasted nice as I washed it down with my third beer.}}

====Usage notes====
* Even in areas such as North America, where deep-fried potato slices are called {{m|en|fries}} or {{m|en|French fries}} and {{m|en|chips}} refer to what are known as {{m|en|crisp|crisps}} in the UK, this dish is still referred to as {{m|en||fish and chips}}.

