delicious.content:
==English==
{{wp|<dab!>}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:delicious<ety:bor<xno:delicious>>|tree=1}}

From {{inh|en|enm|delicious}}, from {{der|en|xno|delicious}}, from {{der|en|fro|delicious|delicious, delicieux}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|deliciosus|dēliciōsus|delicate, delicious}}, from {{m|la|dēliciae|t=delights}}, plural of {{m|la|dēlicia|t=pleasure}}, from {{m|la|delicere|t=to allure, to entice}}, from {{m|la|de-|t=away}} + {{m|la|laciō|t=to lure, deceive}}, from {{der|en|itc-pro|*lakjō|t=to draw, pull}}, of unknown ultimate origin. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|ārlīċ|t=delicious}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈlɪ.ʃəs/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈlɪ.ʃəs/|/də-/|/di-/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-delicious.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪʃəs|s=3}}
* {{hyph|en|de|li|cious}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Pleasing to the sense of [[taste]]; [[tasty]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:delicious}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|year_published=1963|author=w:Edgar Rice Burroughs|title={{w|The Return of Tarzan}}|publisher=Ballantine Books|location=New York|page=120
|passage=It was quite dark by the time Tarzan had gorged himself. Ah, but it had been '''delicious'''! Never had he quite accustomed himself to the ruined flesh that civilized men had served him, and in the bottom of his savage heart there had constantly been the craving for the warm meat of the fresh kill, and the rich, red blood.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=July 8, 1876|author=Christopher E. Benck| title=Recipes for traditional English foods| passage=After the sugar beets are strained, pour in a large amount of honey and let it rest for thirty minutes. This will form the base for our '''delicious''' honey tender.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|figurative}} Pleasing to a person's taste; pleasing to the eyes or mind.
#: {{ux|en|The irony is '''delicious'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1828|author=w:Thomas Keightley|title=The Fairy Mythology|volume=II|location=London|publisher=William Harrison Ainsworth|page=151|passage=Nymphidia is a '''delicious''' piece of airy and fanciful invention.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Patrick Lichfield|title=Courvoisier's Book of the Best|page=230
|passage=But the houses are so '''delicious''' and the way they're townscaped on to hilly bits is absolutely wonderful.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|passage=Jones had not travelled far before he paid his compliments to that beautiful planet, and, turning to his companion, asked him if he had ever beheld so '''delicious''' an evening?}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} Having tremendous [[sex appeal]].

