==English==
{{was wotd|2023|January|8}}

===Pronunciation===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = Thin Noodle.jpg
|caption1 = [[Chinese#Adjective|Chinese]]-[[style#Noun|style]] noodles ({{senseno|en|Q192874}}) [[serve#Verb|served]] in [[soup#Noun|soup]].
|image2 = Kakeudon.jpg
|caption2 = [[Japanese#Adjective|Japanese]] [[udon]] are called noodles ({{senseno|en|Q192874}}) in both the [[United Kingdom]] and the [[United States]].
|image3 = Fusillilunch.jpg
|caption3 = [[fusilli|Fusilli]] is a type of [[Italian#Adjective|Italian]] [[pasta]]; it is called noodles ({{senseno|en|Q192874}}) only in the United States.
|image4 = BC noodle lake fun 2008 cropped (16410173656).jpg
|caption4 = A [[girl#Noun|girl]] [[use#Verb|using]] a noodle ({{senseno|en|Q1428255}}), or [[pool noodle]].
}}

* {{IPA|en|[ˈnuː.dl̩]|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnu.dəl/|[ˈnuː.dl̩]|a=GA,CA,Scotland}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-noodle.ogg|a=California}}
* {{IPA|en|[ˈnʉː.dl̩]|a=AU}}
** {{audio|en|En-au-noodle.ogg|a=Queensland}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːdəl|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|nood|le}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mey-|id=small}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|nl|noedel|t=noodle}},<ref>{{cite-book|author=Nicoline van der Sijs|authorlink=Nicoline van der Sijs|entry=noodles|translator=Piet Verhoeff|title=Cookies, Coleslaw, and Stoops: The Influence of Dutch on the North American Languages|location=Amsterdam|publisher={{w|Amsterdam University Press}}|year=2009|page=134|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qIsDdUSYJMIC&pg=PA134|column=2|isbn=978-90-8964-124-3}}.</ref> or from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{bor|en|de|Nudel|t=piece of pasta, noodle}}; further etymology uncertain, probably a variant of {{m|de|Knödel|t=dumpling}}, from {{der|en|gmh|knödel|t=dumpling; small knot}}, and then either:<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=128173|date=March 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=noodle{{sup|1}}|pos=n}}</ref>

* from {{m|gmh|knode}}, {{m|gmh|knote|t=knot}} (from {{der|en|goh|knodo}}, {{m|goh|knoto|t=knot}}, perhaps ultimately related to {{der|en|gmw-pro|*knappō|t=knob; boy}}) + {{m|gmh|-el|pos={{glossary|diminutive}} {{glossary|suffix}}}}; or
* from {{der|en|lld|menùdli|t=small dough dumpling in soup}}, probably from {{der|en|la|minutulus|t=very small, tiny}} (in the sense of food chopped into small pieces), a diminutive of {{m|la|minūtus|t=diminished; having been diminished}}, the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|minuō|t=to make smaller, diminish, lessen, reduce}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*mey-|t=little, small}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
* {{cog|fr|nouille}}, {{m|fr|noudle}}, {{m|fr|nudeln}}
* {{cog|sv|nudel}}
{{col-bottom}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q192874}} {{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} A [[string#Noun|string]] or [[flat#Adjective|flat]] [[strip#Noun|strip]] of [[pasta]] or other [[dough#Noun|dough]], usually [[cook#Verb|cooked]] (at least [[initially]]) by [[boiling#Noun|boiling]], and [[serve#Verb|served]] in [[soup#Noun|soup]] or in a [[dry#Adjective|dry]] [[form#Noun|form]] [[mix#Verb|mixed]] with a [[sauce#Noun|sauce]] and other [[ingredient]]s.
#: {{ux|en|She slurped a long '''noodle''' up out of her soup.}}
#: {{ux|en|She is cooking '''noodles''' for dinner.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author={{w|Paul Vautin}}|title=Turn It Up!|location=Sydney|publisher=Pan Macmillan Australia |page=148|text=He settled for the '''noodles''' and then after watching ''The Jungle Book'' twice on video it was time for beddy-bys.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003| author=Ma. Romina M. Gonzalez | title=Welostit and Other Stories |page=51| passage=One brought two boxes of instant mami '''noodles'''.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} An [[object#Noun|object]] which is [[long#Adjective|long]] and [[thin#Adjective|thin]] like a noodle ''(sense 1)''.
## {{senseid|en|Q1428255}} {{lb|en|informal}} {{ellipsis of|en|pool noodle|t=a long, [[slender]] tube or rod, [[extrude]]d from [[buoyant]] foam and usually [[brightly]] coloured, used as an exercise tool or toy in [[swimming pool]]s}}.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[dumpling]] cooked by boiling and served in soup; a [[knaidel]] or [[knödel]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} The [[penis]].
# {{lb|en|furry fandom}} A [[long]] and [[slender]] [[dragon]], usually an [[eastern]] dragon.
# {{lb|en|internet slang|endearing|humorous}} A [[borzoi]] dog.

=====Usage notes=====
In British English, the word ''noodle'' ''(sense 1)'' is chiefly used to describe Asian or northern-European food items comprising long, thin strands of dough. In American English, ''noodle'' can also refer to Italian pasta which in British English would only be referred to as {{m|en|pasta}}.<ref name="OED"/><ref>{{cite-web|author=Lynne Murphy|authorlink=Lynne Murphy|title=Noodles|work=Separated by a Common Language: Observations on British and American English by an American Linguist in the UK|titleurl=https://separatedbyacommonlanguage.blogspot.com/2015/08/noodles.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220120141120/https://separatedbyacommonlanguage.blogspot.com/2015/08/noodles.html|archivedate=20 January 2022|date=12 August 2015}}.</ref>

