===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)neh₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|norice}}, from {{der|en|fro|norrice}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|nūtrīcia}}, noun based on {{der|en|la|nūtrīcius||that which nourishes}}, from {{m|la|nūtrīx||wet nurse}}, from {{m|la|nūtriō||to suckle}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A person involved in providing direct care for the [[sick]]:
## {{lb|en|informal}} Anyone performing this role, regardless of [[training]] or profession.
##: {{ux|en|My aunt was my '''nurse''' while I recuperated at home from surgery.}}
## A [[medical]] worker performing this role, typically someone trained to provide such care but having [[credentials]] and [[rank#Noun|rank]] below a [[doctor#Noun|doctor]] or [[physician assistant]].
##: {{ux|en|The '''nurse''' made her rounds through the hospital ward.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|year=1990|author=w:Andrew Davies; w:Michael Dobbs|title=[[w:House of Cards (UK TV show)|House of Cards]]|season=1|number=4
|passage=''Francis Urquhart:'' Right. Mackenzie. Health. No chance of getting him into a [[demo]] at a hospital, I suppose?<br>''Tim Stamper:'' Doesn't go to hospitals any more. Kept getting beaten up by the '''nurses'''... I think he has trouble getting insured now.}}
## {{lb|en|healthcare}} A medical worker, such as a [[registered nurse]], having training, credentials, and rank above a [[nurse assistant]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A person (usually a woman) who takes care of other people’s [[child]]ren.
#: {{ux|en|They hired a '''nurse''' to care for their young boy.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} One who, or that which, brings up, rears, causes to grow, trains, or fosters.
#: {{ux|en|Eton College has been called "the chief '''nurse''' of England's statesmen".}}
#* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage=the '''nurse''' of manly sentiment and heroic enterprise}}
# {{lb|en|horticulture}} A shrub or tree that protects a young plant.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[lieutenant]] or [[first officer]] who takes [[command#Noun|command]] when the [[captain]] is unfit for his place.
# A [[larva]] of certain [[trematode]]s, which produces [[cercaria]]e by [[asexual reproduction]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[wet nurse]].

=====Usage notes=====
* Some speakers consider nurses (medical workers) to be female by default, and thus use the term ''male nurse'' to refer to a man performing the same job.

