From {{inh|en|enm|parent}}, borrowed from {{der|en|xno|parent}}, {{der|en|frm|parent}}, from {{der|en|la|parentem}}, accusative of {{m|la|parēns|id=parent|t=parent}}, present participle of {{m|la|pariō|t=to breed, bring forth}}.

The long vowel of the first syllable, unexpected in a French loan, has been attributed to the English school pronunciation of Latin {{m|la|parēns}},<ref name="Jespersen">{{R:Jespersen:1909-1949|I|4.54|135}}</ref><ref name="Luick">{{R:enm:Luick:1921-1940|I|425|466–467|pageurl=466}}</ref> in which, according to Heck, classical {{m|la|părēns}} was said with either a short or a long vowel.<ref name="Heck1906">{{cite-journal|author=Casimir Heck|title=Die quantitäten der accentvokale in ne. offenen silben mehrsilbiger nicht-germanischer lehnwörter|trans-title=The quantities of stressed vowels in Modern English open syllables of polysyllabic non-Germanic loanwords|journal=[https://archive.org/details/angliazeitschrif29tbuoft Anglia]|volume=29|year=1906|pages=[https://archive.org/details/angliazeitschrif29tbuoft/page/94 94]–95, [https://archive.org/details/angliazeitschrif29tbuoft/page/212 212], [https://archive.org/details/angliazeitschrif29tbuoft/page/251 251]}}</ref> August Western listed the long vowel as an exception in 1885 and again in 1902, when he called it "{{lang|de|scheinbar ohne grund}}" (apparently without reason), but in 1923 he grouped ''parent'' with words whose long vowel came from the Latin school pronunciation.<ref name="Western1885">{{cite-book|author=August Western|title=[https://archive.org/details/englischelautleh00westuoft Englische Lautlehre]|trans-title=English Phonology|location=Heilbronn|publisher=Henninger|year=1885|pages=39–40|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/englischelautleh00westuoft/page/39}}</ref><ref name="Western1902">{{cite-book|author=August Western|title=[https://archive.org/details/englischelautle00westgoog Englische Lautlehre für Studierende und Lehrer]|trans-title=English Phonology for Students and Teachers|edition=2nd|location=Leipzig|publisher=O. R. Reisland|year=1902|pages=44, 49–50}}</ref><ref name="Western1923">{{cite-book|author=August Western|title=[https://www.google.com/books/edition/Englische_Lautlehre_für_Studierende_und/AyBPAAAAYAAJ Englische Lautlehre für Studierende und Lehrer]|trans-title=English Phonology for Students and Teachers|edition=4th|location=Leipzig|publisher=O. R. Reisland|year=1923|page=48|pageurl=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Englische_Lautlehre_für_Studierende_und/AyBPAAAAYAAJ?gbpv=1&pg=PA48}}</ref> Heck posited the long vowel for the 16th century on the strength of Gil's ''pärents'' (see Pronunciation).<ref name="Heck1906"/> {{w|Eric John Dobson|E. J. Dobson}} held instead that the English pronunciation of Latin normally had a short vowel immediately before the stress, and that long variants became more common "because of the general English tendency to have long vowels in stressed open syllables".<ref name="Dobson">{{R:en:Dobson:1957|II|23|495–496|pageurl=495}}</ref>

