* {{q|Early Modern English}} {{w|Peter Levens|Peter Levins}} (1570) marks the stress on the second syllable, ''parént'', beside ''párentage''; Heck took ''parént'' as a Latin stress.<ref name="Levins1570">{{cite-book|author={{w|Peter Levens|Peter Levins}}|title=[https://books.google.com/books?id=s-RiAAAAcAAJ Manipulus Vocabulorum: A Dictionarie of English and Latine Wordes]|location=London|publisher=Henrie Bynneman for John Waley|year=1570|section=sigs. ¶2r, A4r, F1v}}</ref><ref name="Heck1906"/> {{w|Alexander Gill the Elder|Alexander Gil}} (1621), who marks long vowels with two dots, writes ''pärents'' twice but ''parents'' once, in his respelling of a line of {{w|Edmund Spenser|Spenser}}.<ref name="Gil1621">{{cite-book|author={{w|Alexander Gill the Elder|Alexander Gil}}|editor=Otto L. Jiriczek|title=[https://books.google.com/books?id=Q-YXAQAAMAAJ Alexander Gill's Logonomia Anglica: Nach der Ausgabe von 1621 diplomatisch herausgegeben]|trans-title=Alexander Gill's Logonomia Anglica: Edited diplomatically from the 1621 edition|series=Quellen und Forschungen|seriesvolume=90|location=Strassburg|publisher=Trübner|year=1903|origyear=1621|pages=[https://books.google.com/books?id=Q-YXAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA24 24], [https://books.google.com/books?id=Q-YXAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA77 77]–78, [https://books.google.com/books?id=Q-YXAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA108 108]}}</ref> Dobson also records short ''a'' in ''parents'' in {{w|William Bullokar|Bullokar}} (1580); Heck regarded Bullokar's unmarked vowel as uninformative, since Bullokar does not mark the quantity of the stressed vowel in disyllabic loanwords.<ref name="Dobson"/><ref name="Heck1906"/>
