==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpi.pəl/|[ˈpʰi.pəɫ]|[ˈpʰi.pɫ̩]|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-people.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpiː.pəl/|[ˈpʰiː.pɫ̩]|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-people.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|[ˈpi.pɯ̽ɫ]|a=Pacific Northwest,CA}} {{attn|en|check - is this right?}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpipɨl/|/-ɐl/|a=Indic}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpipol/|a=Philippines,and,NNSE}}
* {{homophones|en|papal|peepul|pupil|a=some accents}}
* {{hyphenation|en|peo|ple}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːpəl|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:peple}}
From {{inh|en|enm|puple}}, {{m|enm|peple}}, {{m|enm|peeple}}, from {{derived|en|xno|people}}, from {{derived|en|fro|pueple}}, {{m|fro|peuple}}, {{m|fro|pople}}, from {{derived|en|la|populus|t=a people, nation}}, from {{der|en|itc-ola|populus}}, from earlier {{m|itc-ola|poplus}}, from even earlier {{m|itc-ola|poplos}}, from {{der|en|itc-pro|*poplos|t=army}} of unknown origin. {{doublet|en|pueblo}}. Gradually ousted native {{cog|en|lede}} and, partially, {{m|en|folk}}.

Originally used with singular verbs (e.g. "the people is hungry, and weary, and thirsty, in the wilderness" in the King James Version of 2 Samuel 17:29<ref>{{R:King James Version|2 Samuel|17|29|page=426|column=2|text={{...}}The people ''is'' hungrie, and wearie, and thirſtie in the wilderneſſe.|nodot=1}}</ref>), the plural aspect of ''people'' is probably due to influence from {{cog|enm|lede}}, {{m|enm|leed}}, a plural since [[w:Old English|Old English]] times; see {{m|ang|lēode}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|peeps|peops||slang}}
* {{alter|en|peple||obsolete}}
* {{alter|en|pipple||pronunciation spelling}}
* {{alter|en|ppl|ppl.||Internet slang|or|text messaging}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|persons, plural of person}} {{plural of|en|person}}: a [[body]] of [[person]]s considered generally or collectively; a [[group]] of two or more [[person]]s.
#: {{syn|en|peeps|q1=slang|lede<q:obsolete>|leod<q:obsolete>}}
#: {{ux|en|There were so many '''people''' at the restaurant last night.}}
#: {{ux|en|These six '''people''' are waiting for the bus.}}
#: {{ux|en|Under dictatorship, '''people''' suffered and died.}}
#* '''c. 1607''', plaque recording the [[w:Bristol Channel floods, 1607|Bristol Channel floods]]:
#*: XXII '''people''' was in this parrish drownd.
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|passage="What a charming amusement for young '''people''' this is, Mr. Darcy! There is nothing like dancing after all. I consider it as one of the first refinements of polished society."|chapter=6}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=12|passage=There were many wooden chairs for the bulk of his visitors, and two wicker armchairs with red cloth cushions for superior '''people'''. From the packing-cases had emerged some Indian clubs,{{nb...}}, and all these articles{{...}}made a scattered and untidy decoration that Mrs. Clough assiduously dusted and greatly cherished.}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=1|passage=“{{...}} the awfully hearty sort of Christmas cards that '''people''' do send to other '''people''' that they don't know at all well. You know. The kind that have mottoes like<br><small>&nbsp; Here's rattling good luck and roaring good cheer, / With lashings of food and great hogsheads of beer.</small>{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838|page=11|title=Towards the end of poverty|url=http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21578665-nearly-1-billion-people-have-been-taken-out-extreme-poverty-20-years-world-should-aim|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/HjLZ6|archivedate=2024-09-05|passage=But poverty’s scourge is fiercest below $1.25 (the average of the 15 poorest countries’ own poverty lines, measured in 2005 dollars and adjusted for differences in purchasing power): '''people''' below that level live lives that are poor, nasty, brutish and short.|accessdate=3 June 2013}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 21 2013|author=w:Oliver Burkeman|volume=189|issue=2|page=27|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=Conscious computing: how to take control of your life online
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130824175505/http://www.theguardian.com/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google|archivedate=August 24, 2013
|passage=The dirty secret of the internet is that all this distraction and interruption is immensely profitable. Web companies like to boast about{{nb...}}, or offering services that let you{{...}}"share the things you love with the world" and so on. But the real way to build a successful online business is to be better than your rivals at undermining '''people''''s control of their own attention.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=72–3|title=A punch in the gut|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/le1Hh|archivedate=2023-03-08|url=https://www.economist.com/science-and-technology/2013/06/29/a-punch-in-the-gut|passage=Mostly, the microbiome is beneficial. It helps with digestion and enables '''people''' to extract a lot more calories from their food than would otherwise be possible. Research over the past few years, however, has implicated it in diseases from atherosclerosis to asthma to autism.|accessdate=3 November 2018}}
# {{senseid|en|group of persons, such as a nation}} {{lb|en|countable|collective}} {{U|person|s}} forming or belonging to a particular group, such as a [[nation]], [[class]], [[ethnic]] group, [[country]], [[family]], etc.
#: {{syn|en|collective|community|congregation|folk}}
#: {{cot|en|q1=sometimes synonymous|nation<id:group of persons united by some traits>}}
#: {{collocation|en|a '''people''' apart}}
#: {{collocation|en|an industrious '''people'''}}
#: {{collocation|en|the indigenous '''peoples''' of Europe}}
#: {{collocation|en|the native '''peoples''' of Borneo}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=`So, oh Holly. This '''people''' was an old '''people''' before the Egyptians were.'}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:The New York Review|passage=Other nationalisms aimed at liberating subjugated '''peoples''' who spoke the same language and lived in the same territory.|url=http://www.nybooks.com/articles/archives/2002/dec/19/israelis-palestinians-what-went-wrong/?pagination=false|author=Amos Elon|title=Israelis & Palestinians: What Went Wrong?|date=November 21, 2002|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20121026103143/http://www.nybooks.com/articles/archives/2002/dec/19/israelis-palestinians-what-went-wrong/?pagination=false|archivedate=26 October 2012}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Hafsa Khalil|title=Electric blue tarantula species discovered in Thailand|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/09/26/world/electric-blue-tarantula-discovered-thailand-scn-scli-intl|date=2023-09-26|passage=The team auctioned off the right to name the new species to publicize the find and raise awareness and funds for the indigenous Lahu '''people''' of northern Thailand, a group that Sippawat is part of.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241120180012/https://www.cnn.com/2023/09/26/world/electric-blue-tarantula-discovered-thailand-scn-scli-intl/}}
# {{senseid|en|group of persons subject to a ruler or leader}} A group of persons regarded as being [[servant]]s, [[follower]]s, [[companion]]s or [[subject]]s of a [[ruler]] or [[leader]].
#: {{collocation|en|Moses said, "Let my '''people''' go."}}
#: {{collocation|en|his '''people''' were weary}}
#: {{syn|en|fans|groupies|supporters}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Samuel|8|15|page=415|column=1|text=And Dauid reigned ouer all Iſrael, and Dauid executed iudgement and iuſtice vnto all his '''people'''.}}
#* '''1952,''' ''{{w|Old Testament}}, {{w|Revised Standard Version}}'', Thomas Nelson & Sons, {{w|Book of Isaiah|Isaiah}} 1:3:
#*: The ox knows its owner, and the ass its master's crib; but Israel does not know, my '''people''' does not understand.
# {{senseid|en|one's colleagues or employees}} One's [[colleague]]s or [[employee]]s.
