farmer.content:
{{also|Farmer}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|fermour|t=a steward, bailliff, collector of taxes}},  from {{der|en|fro|fermier|t=a farmer, a lessee, husbandman, bailliff}}, from {{der|en|la-med|firmarius|t=one to whom land is rented, a collector of taxes, deputy}}, from {{m|la|firma}}; equivalent to {{af|en|farm|-er|id2=agent noun}}. Compare {{inh|en|ang|feormere|t=a purveyor of a guild, a supplier of food, a grocer, farmer}}. More at {{l|en|farm}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɑɹ.mɚ/|a=GenAm}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-farmer.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɑː.mə/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-farmer.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)mə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|farm|er}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|[[:Appendix:Glossary#agent noun|agent noun]]}} Someone or something that {{l|en|farm#Verb|farms}}, as:
## A person who works the land and/or who keeps [[livestock]]; anyone engaged in {{l|en|agriculture}} on a {{l|en|farm#Noun}}.
##: {{hypo|en|peasant|q=dated}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|title=An internet of airborne things|date=2012-12-01|volume=405|issue=8813|page=3 (Technology Quarterly)|url=http://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21567193-networking-enthusiasts-dream-building-drone-powered-internet-carry-objects
|passage=A '''farmer''' could place an order for a new tractor part by text message and pay for it by mobile money-transfer. A supplier many miles away would then take the part to the local matternet station for airborne dispatch via drone.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201062641/https://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21567193-networking-enthusiasts-dream-building-drone-powered-internet-carry-objects|archivedate=1 December 2017}}
## {{lb|en|strict|especially|Britain}} More specifically, a farm owner, as distinguished from a {{l|en|farmworker}} or {{l|en|farmhand}} as a hired employee thereof.
##: {{cot|en|farmworker|farmhand}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} One who takes taxes, customs, excise, or other duties, to collect for a certain rate per cent.
#: {{ux|en|a '''farmer''' of the revenues}}
# {{lb|en|historical|mining}} The lord of the field, or one who farms the lot and cope of the crown.
# {{lb|en|Singapore|slang}} A [[regular]] [[person]]; [[someone]] who did not [[receive]] a [[prestigious]] [[scholarship]].
# {{lb|en|dated}} A {{l|en|baby farmer}} (operator of a rural orphanage).

====Usage notes====
''Farmer'' is probably the last occupational descriptor to have been used as a prefix to a surname in everyday usage: e.g. Farmer Brown. This usage was common until the mid 20th century.

