place.content:
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "place"}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|pleace||some English dialects: 18th–19th centuries; Scots: until the 17th century}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpleɪ̯s/|[ˈpʰl̥eɪ̯s]|a=RP,GA,[[w:Standard Canadian English|Standard Canadian]]}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-to place.ogg|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-place.wav|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-place.ogg|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-place.wav|a=Southern England}}
*** {{IPA|en|/ˈpleːs/|[ˈpʰl̥eːs]|a=US,CA,[[w:Monophthongization|monophthongization]]}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈplæ̝ɪ̯s/|[ˈpʰl̥æ̝ɪ̯s]|a=AU,NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪs|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|place}}
* {{homophones|en|plaice}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pleth₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|place}}, conflation of {{inh|en|ang|plæċe|t=place, an open space, street}} and {{der|en|fro|place|t=place, an open space}}, both from {{der|en|la|platēa|t=plaza, wide street}}, from {{der|en|grc|πλατεῖα}}, shortening of {{m|grc|[[πλατεῖα]] [[ὁδός]]|t=broad way}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pleth₂-|t=to spread}}, extended form of {{m|ine-pro|*pleh₂-|t=flat}}. Displaced native {{noncog|ang|stōw}}, {{m|ang|stede}} (partially), and {{m|ang|-ern}}. Compare also {{noncog|en|pleck|t=plot of ground}}, {{noncog|fy|plak|t=place, spot, location}}, {{noncog|nl|plek|t=place, spot, patch}}. {{doublet|en|piatza|piazza|platz|plaza}}.

In the etymological chain from {{m+|la|platēa}}, note {{m+|fro|place}}, which has multiple descendants — including {{cog|de|Platz}}, itself with many descendants (e.g., {{cog|ru|плац}}). Also note a more distant chain node {{cog|grc|πλατύς}}, whence {{cog|en|Plato}} and {{cog|en|plate}} (via Latin).

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|physical}} An [[area]]; [[somewhere]] within an [[area]].
## An [[open]] [[space]], ''[[particularly]]'' a [[city]] [[square]], [[market]] [[square]], or [[courtyard]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|IV|iv|text=Ay, sir, the other squirrel was stolen from me by the hangman's boys in the market-'''place'''}}
## {{lb|en|often|in street names or addresses}} A street, sometimes but not always surrounding a public place, [[square]], or [[plaza]] of the same name.
##: {{ux|en|They live at Westminster '''Place'''.}}
## An [[inhabited]] [[area]]: a [[village]], [[town]], or [[city]].
## [[any|Any]] [[area]] of the [[earth]]: a [[region]].
##: {{ux|en|He is going back to his native '''place''' on vacation.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=22|passage=From another point of view, it was a '''place''' without a soul. The well-to-do had hearts of stone; the rich were brutally bumptious; the Press, the Municipality, all the public men, were ridiculously, vaingloriously self-satisfied.}}
## The [[area]] [[one]] [[occupies]], ''[[particularly]]'' [[somewhere]] to [[sit]].
##: {{ux|en|We asked the restaurant to give us a table with three '''places'''.}}
## The [[area]] where [[one]] [[live]]s: [[one]]'s [[home]], ''[[formerly]]'' {{label|en|chiefly}} [[country]] [[estate]]s and [[farm]]s.
##* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|text=My Lady Dedlock has been down at what she calls, in familiar conversation, her "'''place'''" in Lincolnshire.|2}}
##: {{ux|en|Do you want to come over to my '''place''' later?}}
## An [[area]] of the body, especially the [[skin]].
##: {{ux|en|Which '''place''' hurts the most?}}
## {{label|en|dated|colloquial|euphemism}} An [[area]] to [[urinate]] and [[defecate]]: an [[outhouse]] or [[lavatory]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|author=John Stephen Farmer; et al|title=Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present|volume=V|page=220
|passage=''[[place|Place]]'',... (2) a [[jakes]], or [[house of ease]].}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1951|author=w:William Styron|title=w:Lie Down in Darkness|chapter=ii|page=59
|passage=‘I guess I'll take this opportunity to go to the [[place]]’...<br>‘She means the [[little girls room]].’}}
## {{label|en|obsolete}} An [[area]] to [[fight]]: a [[battlefield]] or the [[contested]] [[ground]] in a [[battle]].
# A [[location]] or [[position]] in [[space]].
#: {{ux|en|I'll check '''place'''s I think she might be.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|act=II|scene=v|passage=In that same '''place''' thou hast appointed me,<br>To-morrow truly will I meete with thee.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=2|passage=What '''place''' can be for us / Within heaven's bound?|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=5|passage=When you're well enough off so's you don't have to fret about anything but your heft or your diseases you begin to get queer, I suppose. And the queerer the cure for those ailings the bigger the attraction. A '''place''' like the Right Livers' Rest was bound to draw freaks, same as molasses draws flies.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=[https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/288354.George_Goodchild George Goodchild]| title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=5| passage=By one o'clock the '''place''' was choc-a-bloc. […] The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two main courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, drawn to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.}}
# Proper or usual position.
#: {{ux|en|A '''place''' for everything, and everything in its '''place'''.}}
# A [[particular]] [[location]] in a [[book]] or [[document]], ''[[particularly]]'' the [[current]] [[location]] of a [[reader]]
#: {{ux|en|I lost my '''place''' when you interrupted me.}}
# {{label|en|obsolete}} A [[passage]] or [[extract]] from a [[book]] or [[document]].
# {{label|en|obsolete|rhetoric}} A [[topic]].
# A [[state of mind]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm in a strange '''place''' at the moment.}}
# {{label|en|chess|obsolete}} A [[chess]] [[position]]; a [[square]] of the [[chessboard]].
# {{lb|en|social}} A [[responsibility]] or [[position]] in an [[organization]].
## A [[role]] or [[purpose]]; a [[station]].
##: {{ux|en|It is really not my '''place''' to say what is right and wrong in this case.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|II|v|passage=I know my '''place''' as I would they should do theirs.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|I|i
|passage=''Escalus''.Esc.I shall desire you, Sir, to giue me leaue<br>To haue free speech with you; and it concernes me<br>To looke into the bottome of my '''place''' :<br>A powre I haue, but of what strength and nature,<br>I am not yet instructed.}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Great Place|passage=Men in great '''place''' are thrice servants.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848| author=Lexington| title=Keeping the mighty honest|url=http://www.economist.com/news/united-states/21583274-new-wave-press-barons-should-not-allow-newspapers-become-niche-products-keeping| passage=The ''[Washington] Post's'' proprietor through those turbulent [Watergate] days, Katharine Graham, held a double '''place''' in Washington’s hierarchy: at once regal Georgetown hostess and scrappy newshound, ready to hold the establishment to account.}}
## The [[position]] as a member of a [[sports]] [[team]].
##: {{ux|en|He lost his '''place''' in the national team.}}
# The [[position]] of a [[contestant]] at the close of a [[competition]].
#: {{ux|en|We thought we would win but only ended up in fourth '''place'''.}}
# {{lb|en|horse racing}} The position of first, second, or third at the finish, especially the second position.
#: {{ux|en|to win a bet on a horse for '''place'''}}
# {{label|en|obsolete}} A [[fortified]] [[position]]: a [[fortress]], [[citadel]], or [[walled]] [[town]].
# Numerically, the [[column]] counting a certain quantity.
#: {{ux|en|three decimal '''places''';&emsp; the hundreds '''place'''}}
# Ordinal relation; position in the order of proceeding.
#: {{ux|en|That's what I said in the first '''place'''!}}
#* '''a. 1788''', Mather Byles, quoted in ''The Life of James Otis'' by William Tudor
#*: In the first '''place''', I do not understand politics; in the second '''place''', you all do, every man and mother's son of you; in the third '''place''', you have politics all the week, pray let one day in the seven be devoted to religion{{...}}
# Reception; effect, implying the making room for.
#* {{RQ:KJV|John|8|37|passage=My word hath no '''place''' in you.}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|market square}} {{l|en|courtyard}}, {{l|en|piazza}}, {{l|en|plaza}}, {{l|en|square}}
* {{sense|somewhere to sit}} {{l|en|seat}}
