===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|es}}

# {{senseid|en|Q23501}} A widely [[cultivated]] [[plant]], {{taxfmt|Solanum lycopersicum|species}}, having [[edible]] [[fruit]].
# {{senseid|en|Q20638126}} The savory [[fruit]] of this plant, most often red when ripe, treated as a [[vegetable]] in [[horticulture]] and [[cooking]].
#: {{ux|en| He was chopping a '''tomato''' to put in the salad.}}
#: {{ux|en| He was eating a '''tomato''' when his boss called him.}}
#: {{syn|en|golden apple|q1=archaic|love apple|q2=archaic|wolf's peach|q3=obsolete}}
#: {{meronyms|en|lycopene}}
#* '''1990''', ''JSG Trading Corp. v. Tray-Wrap, Inc.'', 917 F.2d 75 (2d Cir. 1990)
#*: In common parlance '''tomatoes''' are vegetables, as the Supreme Court observed long ago [see ''Nix v. Hedden'' 149 U.S. 304, 307, 13 S.Ct. 881, 882, 37 L.Ed. 745 (1893)], although botanically speaking they are actually a fruit. [''26 Encyclopedia Americana'' 832 (Int'l. ed. 1981)]. Regardless of classification, people have been enjoying '''tomatoes''' for centuries; even Mr. Pickwick, as Dickens relates, ate his chops in "tomata" sauce.
# {{senseid|en|Q6592940}} A shade of [[red]], the colour typical of a ripe tomato.
#: {{color panel|FF6347}}
# {{senseid|en|physically attractive woman}} {{lb|en|slang}} An attractive [[woman]].
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=April 4, 1999|author=Frank Decaro|authorlink=Frank DeCaro|title=No Longer the Punch-Line State|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1999/04/04/style/no-longer-punch-line-state-lauryn-hill-sopranos-others-are-unapologetic-new.html|section=9|page=1|text=Deborah Harry, the New Wave goddess, is finally admitting -- after all the peroxide and posturing of the 1970's and 80's -- that she's really just a <nowiki>''</nowiki>'''tomato'''<nowiki>''</nowiki> (her word) from Paterson {{...}}.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250702190312/https://www.nytimes.com/1999/04/04/style/no-longer-punch-line-state-lauryn-hill-sopranos-others-are-unapologetic-new.html|archivedate=2 July 2025}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Denny Durbin|title=Lazy Enchiladas: Redefining Success: Tasty Lessons on Love, Life, & Relationships|publisher=Bodega Publishing|isbn=9780980207248|page=13
|passage=When she left the room, I asked Robert, “Who&#39;s the '''tomato'''?” “Marisa. She&#39;s from Mexico.” He had a telltale smile on his face.}}
#* '''2015''' [https://www.bustle.com/articles/116384-19-old-fashioned-compliments-we-should-bring-back 19 Old-Fashioned Compliments We Should Bring Back]
#*: That shirt makes you look like such a glorious '''tomato'''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020|author=Libba Bray|title=The King of Crows|publisher=Little, Brown Books for Young Readers|isbn=9780316532624
|passage=“Who&#39;s the '''tomato'''?” a cop said as Evie walked past. “Her? She&#39;s the stiff&#39;s niece,” another cop answered.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2025|author={{w|Thomas Pynchon}}|title=Shadow Ticket|publisher=Jonathan Cape|page=19|passage=When did this happen last, a '''tomato''' he's hardly met going to so much trouble? Ever?}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A stupid act or person.

