==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|so}}, {{m|enm|swo}}, {{m|enm|zuo}}, {{m|enm|swa}}, {{m|enm|swe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|swā}}, {{m|ang|swǣ}}, {{m|ang|swē|t=so, as, the same, such, that}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*swā}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*swa}}, {{m|gem-pro|*swē|t=so}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*swé|*swē, *swō}} (reflexive pronomial stem). Cognate with {{cog|sco|sae|t=so}}, {{cog|stq|so|t=so}}, {{cog|fy|sa|t=so}}, {{cog|nl|zo|t=so}}, {{cog|nds-de|so|t=so}}, {{cog|de|so|t=so}}, {{cog|da|så|t=so}}, {{cog|nn|so|t=so}}, {{cog|sv|så|t=so, such that}}, {{cog|fo|so|t=so}}, {{cog|is|svo|t=so}}, {{cog|itc-ola|suad|t=so}}, {{cog|sq|sa|t=how much, so, as}}, {{cog|grc|ὡς|t=as}}, {{cog|ur|سو|tr=sō|t=hence}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|sō|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/səʊ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Pvanp7-so.wav|a=UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/siː/|a=Northumbrian}}
*** {{IPA|en|/sə/|a=unstressed}}
* {{enPR|sō|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/soʊ/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-so.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|[soː]|a=CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/so/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/soː/|a=Wales,non-toe-tow}}
* {{IPA|en|/sʌo/|a=Dublin,Local}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊ|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|seau|soe|soh|;|sew|sow<aa:toe-tow>}} {{q|sense 2}} [[soy]] {{qualifier|some non-standard dialects}}

====Conjunction====
{{en-head|conj}}

# {{senseid|en|in order that}} {{n-g|Reduced form of '[[so that]]', used to express purpose}}; [[in order that]].
#: {{ux|en|I got an earlier train to work '''so''' I'd have plenty of time to prepare for the meeting.}}
#: {{ux|en|Eat your broccoli '''so''' you can have dessert.}}
# {{senseid|en|therefore, thus}} As a result; for that reason; [[therefore]]; because of this; due to this.
#: {{ux|en|I was hungry, '''so''' I asked if there was any more food.}}
#: {{ux|en|He ate too much cake and '''so''' he fell ill.}}
#: {{ux|en|He wanted a book, '''so''' he went to the library.}}
#: {{ux|en|“I need to go to the bathroom.” ― “'''So''' go!”}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=Thinks I to myself, &ldquo;Sol, you're run off your course again. This is a rich man's summer &lsquo;cottage&rsquo;{{nb...}}.&rdquo; '''So''' I started to back away again into the bushes. But I hadn't backed more'n a couple of yards when I see something so amazing that I couldn't help scooching down behind the bayberries and looking at it.}}
# {{n-g|Used to connect previous conversation or events to the following question.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''So''' how does this story end?}}
# {{n-g|Used to introduce a rhetorical question.}}
#: {{ux|en|“We'd like to visit but I don't know if we can afford a hotel.” — “'''So''' who's staying in a hotel? Stay with us.”}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[provided|Provided]] that; on condition that; as long as.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|III|1|text=''Speed''. ‘Item: She doth talk in her sleep.’<br>''Launce''. It’s no matter for that, '''so''' she sleep not in her talk.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=18|book=II|passage=As we cal money not onely that which is true and good, but also the false; '''so''' it be currant.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Areopagitica|page=35|text={{...}} though all the windes of doctrin were let loose play upon the earth, '''so''' Truth be in the field, we do injuriously by licencing and prohibiting to misdoubt her strength.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1743|author={{w|Robert Drury (sailor)|Robert Drury}}|title=The Pleasant, and Surprizing Adventures of Mr. Robert Drury, during his Fifteen Years Captivity on the Island of Madagascar|url=https://archive.org/details/pleasantandsurp00drurgoog|page=111|location=London
|passage=I went away very well satisfy’d, not caring where I was sent, '''so''' it was but out of his Sight; for he now became more my Aversion than ever.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Chiefly in North American use, a [[comma]] or [[pause]] is often used before the conjunction when used in the sense ''as a result''. (A similar meaning can often be achieved by using a [[semicolon]] or [[colon]] (without the '''''so'''''), as for example: ''He drank the poison; he died.'')

The use of ''so'' to begin sentences, such as replies to questions, where there is no relevant sense of ''in order that'' or ''for that reason'', has become increasingly common over the early part of the 21st century.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{s|in order that}} {{l|en|so that}}, {{l|en|that}}

=====Translations=====
{{see translation subpage|Conjunction}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# To the (explicitly stated) extent.
#: {{ux|en|It was '''so''' hot outside that all the plants died.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was '''so''' good, they hired him on the spot.}}
#: {{ux|en|You behaviour '''so''' incensed me that I even thought of firing you.}}
#: {{ux|en|It was '''so''' cold a day that I could hardly breathe outside.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''So''' ridiculously did I do in the exam that mom grounded me for a month.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=Thinks I to myself, &ldquo;Sol, you're run off your course again. This is a rich man's summer &lsquo;cottage&rsquo;{{nb...}}.&rdquo; So I started to back away again into the bushes. But I hadn't backed more'n a couple of yards when I see something '''so''' amazing that I couldn't help scooching down behind the bayberries and looking at it.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21581980-latest-research-suggests-humans-are-not-warriors-their-genes-after-all-old|title=Old soldiers?
|passage=Whether modern, industrial man is less or more warlike than his hunter-gatherer ancestors is impossible to determine. The machine gun is '''so''' much more lethal than the bow and arrow that comparisons are meaningless.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130822003848/http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21581980-latest-research-suggests-humans-are-not-warriors-their-genes-after-all-old}}
# To the (implied) [[extent]].
#: {{ux|en|I need a piece of cloth '''<u>so</u>''' long. [{{=}} ''this'' long]}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=We drove back to the office with some concern on my part at the prospect of '''so''' large a case. Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke.}}
# [[very|Very]] (positive or negative clause).
#: {{ux|en|I feel '''so''' much better now.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''so''' nearly lost my temper.}}
#: {{ux|en|It’s not '''so''' bad. [i.e. it's acceptable]}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I
|passage=Captain Edward Carlisle{{...}}felt a curious sensation of helplessness seize upon him as he met her steady gaze,{{nb...}}; he could not tell what this prisoner might do. He cursed the fate which had assigned such a duty, cursed especially that fate which forced a gallant soldier to meet '''so''' superb a woman as this under handicap '''so''' hard.}}
# Very much.
#: {{ux|en|But I '''<u>so</u>''' want to see the Queen when she visits our town!}}
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=Molly the dairymaid came a little way from the rickyard, and said she would pluck the pigeon that very night after work. She was always ready to do anything for us boys; and we could never quite make out why they scolded her so for an idle hussy indoors. It seemed '''so''' unjust.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|publisher=Archie Comics|title=Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles #5|text=I '''so''' wanted to be Jess Harley again.|year=1989}}
#* {{quote-av|en|actor={{w|Andrea Baker|Andrea Taylor}}|title=w:Totally Spies!|season=2|number=9|text=Yeah! Not eating is '''so''' 90’s!|role=Clover|episode=I, Dude|writers=Michael Stokes|network=w:Teletoon|publisher=w:Marathon Media|date=Apr 16 2003}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} [[at all]] (negative clause).
##: {{ux|en|That is '''<u>so</u>''' not true!}}
# In the same manner or to the same extent as aforementioned; [[likewise]], [[also]].
#: {{usex|en|As above, '''so''' below.}}
#: {{ux|en|Just as you have the right to your free speech, '''so''' I have the right to mine.}}
#: {{ux|en|Many people say she's the world's greatest athlete, but I don't think '''so'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|"I can count backwards from one hundred." "'''So''' can I."}}
#: {{usex|en|He wants to eat now. '''So''' does she.}}
#: {{ux|en|People wanting to leave now may do '''so'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Pyle Robin Hood|chapter=V|passage="Good morrow to thee, jolly fellow," quoth Robin, "thou seemest happy this merry morn." ¶ "Ay, that am I," quoth the jolly Butcher, "and why should I not be '''so'''? Am I not hale in wind and limb? Have I not the bonniest lass in all Nottinghamshire? And lastly, am I not to be married to her on Thursday next in sweet Locksley Town?"}}
