#* {{quote-book|en|year=1817|title=Tales of Real Life, Or, Scenes in Ireland |author=Mrs. Edgworth  |page= |ISBN= |passage=I have found it so, for whenever I saw the meal and potatoes running low, I spared them, and '''kitchened''' them all I could, and never was run out of them till the new came in. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1851 |title=Tales of the Borders and of Scotland |author=John Mackay Wilson  |page= |ISBN= |passage=I "'''kitchened'''" my loaf, as they say in Scotland, with a pennyworth of butter, and occasionally with lettuce or a few radishes in their season ; and the beverage with which I regaled myself, after my meals, was a glass of water from the nearest pump. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|title=Fishing Incidents and Adventures |author=Malcolm Ferguson  |page= |ISBN= |passage=The green hill slopes are dotted over with sheep and lambs nibbling away at their morning meal, '''kitchened''' with blabs of sparkling dew, and higher up the mountain side we hear the " cootie moorcock's coothy craw."  }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1907|title=Scotland of to-day  |author=Thomas Finlayson Henderson; Francis Watt  |page=358 |ISBN= |passage=This was seasoned with salt and sometimes '''kitchened''' with butter. }}
