====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|uncommon}} To [[feed]]: to provide with soup or a meal.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1845| title=Tales of the Colonies: Or, The Adventures of an Emigrant |author=Charles Rowcroft | page=432| passage=I'm blessed if I've heard about any thing but kangaroo-tail soup all the while I was at Launceston. They '''souped''' me there night and day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1896| title=Readiana; Comments on Current Events |author=Charles Reade | page=2| passage=Now laughing together thaws our human ice; long before Swindon it was a talking match, —at Swindon who so devoted as Captain Dolignan,—he handed them out—he '''souped''' them,—he tough-chickened them,—he brandied and cochinealed one, and he brandied and burnt-sugared the other;}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1904|month=October|title=East is East and West is West|journal=The Vassar Miscellany|volume=34|issue=1|page=236
|passage="I was so mad, I let him wait half an hour to-night before I '''souped''' him."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Diza Sauers|title=Historama|page=152
|passage=She cooked huge stock pots and '''souped''' her dogs once a day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=C Mark Chapoton|title=A Tale of Two Iditarods|page=34|passage=I '''souped''' the dogs, and went in for a bite. I ended up going back out and making my pups a full meal, then went back in and pigged out myself.}}
# {{lb|en|photography}} To [[develop]] (film) in a (chemical) developing [[solution]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1970|month=December|journal=The Rotarian|volume=117|number=6|page=31
|passage=That girl Vivienne, by the way, once worked as a secretary in the workshop of The Rotarian, began "'''souping'''" her own snapshots at home, went from there to top rank as a New York color photographer specializing in small children {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=Ruth Jean Dale|title=Society Page
|passage="Then perhaps it won't surprise you to learn Annie's taking over the Sunday social column," Roz said. "You photo-guys'll be '''souping''' her film."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Edward Gorman|title=Cold Blue Midnight
|passage=And her camera position had been completely out of his sight. Satisfied that she'd gotten everything she'd needed - much more, in fact - she went back inside and got to work. Jill had '''souped''' her first photographs while she'd been on {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Jock Lauterer|title=Community Journalism: A Personal Approach|page=242|passage=By 6 pm Beau and I are back at the paper, '''souping''' the film, when Woody rushes into the room.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[proselytize]] by feeding the impoverished as long as they listen to one's preaching.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855| title=A Digest of the Evidence, Taken Before the Select Committee of the House of Lords |author=William Le Poer Trench | page=280| passage=Was the priest who denounced those books of the National Board as "'''souping''' books" the patron of a national school?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864| title=Irish Diamonds, Or, A Chronicle of Peterstown |author=Emily Bowles|page=203| passage="'''Souping'''" in Peterstown came to an end, and Una had enough to do with her full school and ignorant scholars to deaden the sting of her grief for the time. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1871| title=Hansard's Parliamentary Debates |author=Thomas Curson Hansard | page=1751| passage=It was suggested that the briefs should be distributed generally; but they could not be spread as they were at Manchester, Liverpool, and Leeds, where briefs where "'''souped'''" out.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1877| title=The Hon. Miss Ferrard|author= Mary Hartley | page=101| passage=Yes, and it was all done in pure charity, no '''souping''' swaddling mixture whatever. The Fitz-Ffoulkes had none of that about them .}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1887|month=January|page=36|work=Time: A Monthly Magazine |title=November in Kerry|author=|volume=5|number=|passage=Before long we passed a Scripture-reader (such the driver said he was), reading a book as a priest does his breviary. I though him not out of place; for anything madder than the whole system of "'''souping'''" it is hard to imagine. In Kerry you see signs of it here and there, as you do in Connemara.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891| title=Hansard's Parliamentary Debates |author=Thomas Curson Hansard | page=838| passage=I ask, Sir, what right has the hon. Member to call any Protestant minister a '''souping''' parson?.}}

===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|en|sup}}
From {{inh|en|enm|soupen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sūpan||to sup, sip}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sūpaną}}. More at {{l|en|sup}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{alternative form of|en|sup|t=to [[sip]]; to take a small amount of food or drink into the mouth, especially with a spoon}}.

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|soupe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sūpe|t=sup; draught}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{alternative form of|en|sup|t=a [[sip]]; a small amount of food or drink}}.

===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|enm|swopen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|swāpan|t=to sweep}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*swaipaną|t=to sweep}}. More at {{m|en|sweep}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|soop}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[sweep]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1597| title=Virgidemiarum|author=Joseph Hall| page=| passage=He vaunts his voice upon an hired stage, With high-set steps and princely carriage, Now '''souping''' in side robes of royalty. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1598| title=The Scourge of Villanie|author=John Marston| page=| passage=Methinks I hear swart Martius cry, '''Souping''' along in war's fein'd maskerie, By Lais starrie front he'll forthwith die.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1808| chapter=Quo Vadis? A Censure of Travel| author=Joseph Hall; Josiah Pratt | title=Miscellaneous works |editor=| page=238| passage=We can tell of those cheap-dieted men, that live about the head of Ganges, without meat, without mouths, feeding only upon air at their nostrils; or of those headless eastern people, that have their eyes in their breast; a mis-conceit arising from their fashion of attire, which I have sometimes seen; or those Coromandae, of whom Pliny speaks, that cover their whole body with their ears; or of the persecutors of St. Thomas of Canterbury, whose posterity, if we believe the confident writings of Degrassalius, are born with long and hairy tails, '''souping''' after them; which, I imagine, gave occasion to that proverbial jest, wherewith our mirth uses to upgraid the Kentish; or of Amazons; or Pigmies; or Satyrs;}}

===Etymology 5===
From {{inh|en|enm|soupen}}, {{m|enm|suppen}}, from {{der|en|xno|super}}, from {{m|fro|supe}}, {{m|fro|soupe|t=soup}} + {{m|fro|-er|pos=verb-forming suffix}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{rare form of|en|sup|t=to take [[supper]]}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Mark Napier (historian)|Mark Napier]]|quotee=[[w:James Melville of Halhill|James Melville]]|chapter=III|title=Memoirs of [[w:John Napier|John Napier of Merchiston]], His Lineage, Life, and Times, with a History of the Invention of Logarithms|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:Blackwood (publishing house)|William Blackwood]]; London: Thomas Cadell|year=a. 1618|year_published=1834|page=116|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/memoirsofjohnnap00napi/page/116/mode/1up|passage=When I cam that tym to the court, I fand my Lord Due of Orkney sitting at his supper. He said I had bene a gret stranger, desyring me to sit down and '''soup''' with him. The Erie of Huntly, the ''justice-clark'', and dyvers uthers, wer sitten at the table with him. I said that I had already '''souped'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Moore|editors=Wilfred S. Dowden; Barbara G. Bartholomew; Joy L. Linsley|chapter=1842&#x2060;<!--to not display as a chapter number-->|title=The Journal of Thomas Moore|volume=5 (1836–1842)|location=Newark, Del.|publisher=w:University of Delaware Press; London; Toronto, Ont.: {{w|Associated University Presses}}|date=12 May 1842|year_published=1988|page=2245|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/journalofthomasm0005moor/page/2245/mode/1up|isbn=0-87413-257-6|passage=Breakfasted with Rogers, who asked me to dine to-day and tomorrow—promised for both days—Most agreeably surprised by a note from my friend Tom Boyse, who is in London with his sister—Called upon them & found to my disappointment they were to be off again so soon as tomorrow—Offered myself for dinner to-day though with the fear of Rogers before my eyes, to whom I wrote to announce my defection and its cause—Only the Boyses themselves, young Carew (Lord Carew’s son) who '''''souped''''' with us, being obliged to set off immediately to the new Opera—{{...|nospace=1}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Hans Hermann Behr|H[ans] H[ermann] Behr]]|chapter=Ethnology|title=The Hoot of the Owl|location=San Francisco, Calif.|publisher=A. M. Robertson|year=1904|page=104|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hootofowl00behrrich/page/104/mode/1up|passage=For weeks I had breakfasted, lunched, dined, and '''souped''' on mutton.}}

